WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.992
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.992 --> 00:00:08.730
I'd like to ask you about GPA.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:13.505
Stanford says that there’s no minimum GPA,
but can you get in with a low GPA?

00:00:13.505 --> 00:00:17.836
I’d also like to know how you
evaluate GPAs outside of the US?

00:00:17.836 --> 00:00:21.250
&gt;&gt; Both of those questions come
up from applicants often as well.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:27.050
Again, the GPA is just one indicator, but
this one is about a four year period of

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:31.450
time often, a three or four year
period of time, so we do look at that.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.750
For us, if you think about the GPA,
we know that

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:38.990
it's going to vary by institution,
the level of a GPA, also even by major.

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:42.950
Sometimes I find that highly rigorous
quantitative majors are sometimes

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.510
more science oriented, on average
students’ GPAs will be lower there.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:49.080
So that’s something we’re
taking into consideration.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.304
In addition,
if you think about international schools,

00:00:53.304 --> 00:00:57.021
we don’t convert their
GPAs into US conversions.

00:00:57.021 --> 00:00:58.573
We actually look at the country and

00:00:58.573 --> 00:01:01.780
try to be familiar with what's
their educational system.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.560
And recognize that oftentimes
international GPAs

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.390
are less inflated than US GPAs may be.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:11.550
So all of that is for
you to realize that we're trying to do our

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:15.120
best to understand what they looks
like in your particular context.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.072
That being said, if you look at
you transcript, and take it and

00:01:18.072 --> 00:01:20.355
look at at it with fresh
eyes as an applicant, and

00:01:20.355 --> 00:01:23.416
you see a point in time, a semester,
quarter, whatever it was,

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:27.236
where you don't think it reflects your
true capabilities, tell us about that.

00:01:27.236 --> 00:01:30.638
We have an additional information section,
it could've been health,

00:01:30.638 --> 00:01:34.710
it could've been a personal situation, but
we're looking for reasons to admit you,

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.130
not for reasons to deny.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:39.855
So any context that you can give us to
help understand what your trajectory has

00:01:39.855 --> 00:01:41.980
been would be really helpful.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.503
&gt;&gt; That's great, I like the framing
of that too, we're looking for

00:01:44.503 --> 00:01:45.908
reasons to admit us, not to deny us.

00:01:45.908 --> 00:01:46.778
&gt;&gt; Excatly.
&gt;&gt; I think that's great.

00:01:46.778 --> 00:01:49.961
&gt;&gt; And no applicant is going to be
outstanding in every dimension, [LAUGH].

00:01:49.961 --> 00:01:51.401
So you put your best foot forward and

00:01:51.401 --> 00:01:53.470
know that those strengths
are the important things

